# Deploy Workflow

This project uses Vercel connected to GitHub. Pushing to `main` deploys to production. Pushing to any other branch deploys to a Vercel preview URL only.

## Default behavior — local validation, then production when ready

The maintainer validates changes **locally** (`npm run build`, `npm run dev` as needed) before shipping.

When the user asks to commit, push, or deploy changes **without** asking for a preview-only branch, give commands that assume they are satisfied after local checks:

```bash
npm run build
git add .
git commit -m "your commit message"
git push origin main
```

After giving these commands, add a note like:

> This pushes to **`main`** and deploys **production** on Vercel. Confirm locally first (`npm run build` / manual QA on `npm run dev`).

Do **not** default to creating a preview branch unless the user asks for preview-only deployment.

## Preview deployments (optional)

When the user explicitly wants a **Vercel preview URL** without merging to `main`, use a branch push:

```bash
git checkout -b <descriptive-branch-name>
git add <specific files>
git commit -m "your commit message"
git push origin <branch-name>
```

After giving these commands, add a note like:

> This pushes to **preview only**. Vercel will deploy a preview URL.

## Branch naming (preview branches)

Use short, descriptive kebab-case names based on what the feature or fix is. Examples:

- `draft-mode`
- `marketing-copy-update`
- `fix/date-display`

## Important rules

- Prefer **`npm run build`** (and local QA) before **`git push origin main`**.
- Never combine unrelated preview and production instructions in one response unless the user asks for both options explicitly.
- **Bracket segments in paths are globs in zsh** — quote paths like `'app/dashboard/event/[id]/page.tsx'` when using `git add` with literals.
